      {
        "review_id": 176585808,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r176585808-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "Interesting but beware of the cost!",
        "text": "I recently finished a 2 week work project in Eastern Nepal and had only 1/2 day to tour Kathmandu.  The Pashupatinath Temple was one of the sites I saw; spent about 45 min there.  First, they used to charge 250, then 500 and now, as of April, 1000 rupee to get inside.  About 10 USD.  The Temple itself is only open to Hindus but you can wander around the grounds.  I hired a guide because the guide I came with did not know about the history of this temple.  The temple guide was excellent.  He was very informative and spoke English well.   It was not clear to me he worked for the temple; I thought he was on his own (though did produce an authorized govt guide card).  He charged me another 1000 rupee.  While there a number of women selling beads tried to hit me up.  I took a photo of one place and had to pay a tip.  Then had my photo with some holy men.  Before the photo the guide warned they would be looking for a donation.  Fair enough.  After the photos, I asked the guide how much.  He did not advise.  I gave 50 rupee and was told this was not enough.  So I asked (again) what they wanted.  He said 100 each!  That would have been another 6 USD.  I handed over about half that amount---300 rupees.  Then, the end of the tour, the guide \"suggested\" I also tip him.  He suggested i give him 5 euros (a note I had in my wallet, which he apparently had noticed).  Ridiculous as that is about 7 USD.  After having already forked out 23-24 USD, I was just done.  So I did not tip.  The guide was not very happy and neither was the other guide I had hired but could not tell me anything.  He made some comment like, \"well he just went into the tourist office, they probably pay him.\"   I had such a fabulous experience in Eastern Nepal where are no tourists and people are not tainted.   This cast a shadow over my experience.   The other part was seeing other tourists take photos of families grieving while their relative was being cremated.  Over the top in terms of disrespect.   I could not wait to leave this dirty, crowded city of over 7 million people.",
